  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe main story this week is the incoming heatwave beginning on\nThursday. An upper ridge currently located to the east will\nstrengthen and move westward through this weekend. There is strong\nagreement among models that 500 mb heights will peak somewhere\naround 597 to 600 dm on Saturday, setting the stage for the \nhottest temperatures of the year so far for inland areas. High \ntemperatures ranging from a few to 8 degrees above normal on \nThursday will combine with slightly above normal lows to create \nmoderate to locally major HeatRisk for inland areas. By the\nweekend, both high and low temperatures increase to 5 to 12 \ndegrees above normal. Peak temperatures this weekend will range \nfrom the 80s in coastal areas to 115 to 120 in the low deserts. \n90s expected in the western valleys and mountains below 7000 feet,\nlow 100s in eastern San Diego Valleys, and 100s to near 110 in \nthe Inland Empire and High Desert. This will create moderate \nHeatRisk for coastal areas, moderate to major HeatRisk for inland \nvalleys and mountains, and major to extreme HeatRisk in the \ndeserts. Some daily record highs are expected to fall in the \nmountains and deserts this weekend.\n\nAs a consequence of the building upper ridge, the marine layer is\nexpected to become more shallow and confined to coastal areas by \nlate week. Minor visibility reductions will become possible for \ncoastal areas each morning this weekend. Additionally, increasing\ninversion strength may allow for marine layer clouds to persist\ninto the afternoon along the immediate coastline. This adds\nuncertainty to current high temperature forecasts. Current highs \nare expected to range 5 to 10 degrees above normal (generally low\n80s) along the coastline this weekend. If clouds are present in \nthe afternoons, highs will be closer to seasonal averages.\n\nThe ridge is expected to weaken early next week, bringing a\nslight cooling trend. However, high temperatures are generally\nexpected to remain above average. Thus, moderate heatrisk will \nlikely continue for inland valleys/mountains with major HeatRisk \nin the deserts. Model guidance diverges more significantly from \nmid-next week onward, though there is some indication that a more\nfavorable monsoon pattern will re-emerge.",
